---
title: Swedish Meatballs
description: Soft pork and beef meatballs lightly spiced with nutmeg and allspice, browned and then simmered in a creamy beef gravy made in the same pan.
author: Nagi Maehashi
source: https://www.recipetineats.com/swedish-meatballs/
course: main
cuisine: scandinavian
diet: meat
difficulty: easy
servings: 5
yield: 25%meatballs
time: 50m
prep time: 20m
cook time: 30m
tags: [beef, pork, pan-fried, one-pan]
---

= Meatballs =

Grate the @onion{1}(small) on a #box grater{} and scrape the onion and all its juices into a #bowl. Add the @white sandwich bread{2%slices}(crusts removed, cut into small cubes) and mix well. The onion juice should make the bread soggy; if it doesn't, add @?milk{1%tbsp}. Leave to soak ~{1%minutes}.

Add the @ground beef{300%g}, @ground pork{300%g}(or more beef), @egg{1}, @nutmeg{0.25%tsp}(freshly grated, or ground), @ground allspice{0.25%tsp}, @black pepper{0.25%tsp} and @salt{0.75%tsp}. Mix well.

Scoop heaped tablespoons of the mixture onto the work surface (a lever ice cream scoop is fastest), 25 to 30 in total. Portion out all the mixture first, then roll each into a ball, so they come out evenly sized.

= Browning =

Heat @neutral oil{1%tbsp} in a #large skillet{} over medium-high heat. Brown half the meatballs all over, ~{3%minutes}; they should still be raw inside. Move them to a #plate and brown the second batch.

If there is a lot of oil left, pour off the excess, but keep the brown bits in the pan: they are the main flavour of the gravy. Lower the heat to medium.

= Gravy =

Melt @butter{40%g}(unsalted) in the skillet, stir in @plain flour{30%g} and cook ~{1%minutes}.

While stirring, add about a quarter of the @beef stock{500%ml}(salt-reduced, or chicken stock); it will thicken quickly. Gradually add the rest, stirring as you go. Switch to a #whisk if needed to get it lump-free.

Once the liquid is simmering, return the meatballs and any juices pooled on the plate. Raise the heat slightly to keep a rapid simmer and cook ~{8%minutes} (up to 10), stirring occasionally, until the liquid thickens into a thin gravy.

Stir in @cream{125%ml}(heavy, about 35% fat), simmer ~{2%minutes} more and take off the heat.

Serve with @?mashed potato{to serve} and a spoonful of @?lingonberry jam{to serve} on the side.

> No allspice: use mixed spice, or equal parts nutmeg, cinnamon and ground cloves.

> Sour cream gives a slight tang, like a stroganoff. Yoghurt or lower-fat cream also work, but the gravy needs an extra minute or two to thicken and is less rich.

> Don't bake the meatballs instead of frying: the pan drippings are what make the gravy.